.\" mount.1 -- maux's mount(1), in the V7 man(1) shape. An original
.\" page (this project's own text), so it carries no third-party
.\" notice -- MIT like the code, same posture as tty.1. Rendered at
.\" build time to build/man/MOUNT.TXT and staged on the play image
.\" as /usr/man/MOUNT: `man mount`.
.TH MOUNT 1 "October 10, 2026" maux
.SH NAME
mount \- list the mounted volumes, mount a floppy, or mount a disk image
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B mount
.br
.B mount
.I /dev/sNdM
.br
.B mount
.RB [ \-r ]
.I image
.SH DESCRIPTION
With no operand
.I mount
prints one line per volume shown under
.BR /mnt ,
for example
.PP
.nf
/dev/s7d1 on /mnt/MAUX
.fi
.PP
A fast unit (SmartPort, hard disk) is always mounted. A 5.25-inch
Disk ][ appears only after it has been mounted here, so listing
never touches an empty drive. The boot volume is listed first; the
rest follow in slot, then drive, order.
.PP
With one operand,
.I /dev/sNdM
(slot N, drive M),
.I mount
reads that one unit once and, if it holds a ProDOS volume, marks it
mounted: it then appears in
.BR /mnt ,
.IR df (1)
and this listing, and its files are under
.BR /mnt/NAME .
The call is the one deliberate poll of a Disk ][ in the system. A
drive with no readable volume fails with the MLI's own reason and
nothing is mounted. Mounting a fast unit succeeds and does nothing.
.PP
With one operand that is not a unit,
.I mount
treats it as a disk IMAGE: a ProDOS tree file (a
.B .po
or
.B .hdv
over 128K) on any mounted volume. The kernel attaches it as unit
.B /dev/s3d2
(slot 3, drive 2: the slot ProDOS reserves for /RAM, which maux
never uses), and the volume inside appears under
.B /mnt
like any other, so
.IR dd (1)
and
.IR nulib2 (1)
work on
.B /dev/s3d2
with nothing new. What is written through the unit lands in the
image file, block for block, straight on the host volume's disk, so
the image is current outside maux as soon as the write returns. The
image file stays open for as long as it is
mounted: it cannot be removed, renamed or overwritten, and
.IR umount (1)
closes it.
.PP
There is NO
.IR losetup :
the unit is fixed, there is one image at a time, and
.I mount
is the only verb. A second image is refused as "busy", and so is one
that lives inside the first. An image is refused, with the reason,
when it is a seedling or a sapling (under 128K) or a directory, when
it is a 2IMG container the loop cannot serve (see below), and when it
is not a ProDOS volume at all. While
an image is mounted
the volume that holds it cannot be unmounted.
.PP
A SPARSE image (a file with blocks ProDOS never allocated) is made
dense first: unless
.B \-r
is given,
.I mount
rewrites every all-zero block of the file so that ProDOS allocates it
(the loop driver cannot allocate), and says "densified N blocks" on
the way. This writes to the image file, and takes about 90000
cycles per block of the image; a dense image costs nothing.
.PP
With
.B \-r
the image is mounted read-only, and is never written, densified
included: the unit answers "write protected" to a write, holes read
as zeros. An image whose access byte is not write-enabled is mounted
read-only without
.BR \-r .
A 2IMG container (a
.B .2mg
file: ProDOS order, a header of 1 to 64 bytes, whole blocks) is always
mounted read-only; convert it to a
.B .po
for read-write. A write that fails (to a read-only image, or one the
host refuses) is reported by
.IR umount (1).
.PP
.IR Bye (1)
works with an image mounted: it is detached for the trip and attached
again afterwards.
.SH FILES
/dev/sNdM       the units
.br
/dev/s3d2       the mounted image, while there is one
.br
/mnt/NAME       a mounted volume
.SH "SEE ALSO"
umount(1), df(1), ls(1)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Exit status is 0 on success, 1 if the unit or image could not be
mounted ("no device connected", "not a ProDOS volume", "not a
mountable unit", "busy", "no such file", "disk full" while densifying,
"2IMG layout not supported", "not a tree file", "needs ProDOS 2.4"),
2 for a usage error.
.SH BUGS
One image at a time. The write-failure bookkeeping reads ProDOS 2.4's
own cells: on another ProDOS the mount is refused.
